src/nist_csf_v2/schema/*.yaml, src/nist_csf_v2/datamodel/ and project/
model the Cybersecurity Framework (CSF) 2.0, which the National
Institute of Standards and Technology (NIST) publishes as Cybersecurity
White Paper (CSWP) 29, https://doi.org/10.6028/NIST.CSWP.29 (February
26, 2024). They are this project's rendering, written in its own words
with NIST as the source of the definitions; they are not NIST's, and
their use does not imply endorsement by NIST.

CSWP 29 carries no licence statement of its own and refers readers to
NIST's Technical Series policies. NIST's statement on its works, read
on 2026-10-29 at
https://www.nist.gov/open/copyright-fair-use-and-licensing-statements-srd-data-software-and-technical-series-publications,
says that a NIST Technical Series publication is a work of the United
States Government, not subject to copyright protection in the United
States, but may be subject to copyright in other countries.

NIST's CSF catalog in OSCAL
---------------------------

src/nist_csf_v2/schema/oscal-content/ holds a copy of the catalog of
CSF 2.0 that NIST publishes in the Open Security Controls Assessment
Language (OSCAL), retrieved from
https://github.com/usnistgov/oscal-content/tree/f0de6d8d8c059d374f15e0ae07508d82e370fc1d/nist.gov/CSF/v2.0/json
(the v1.6.0 release), beside NIST's LICENSE.md from the root of the same
commit. Both files are identical to their counterparts there, and
scripts/check_upstream_release.py checks that they stay so. NIST's work
is in the public domain within the United States, and NIST dedicates it
to the worldwide public domain under CC0 1.0. The tests read the catalog
to check the schema against it; nothing in this repository changes it.

